  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "state": "documented",
      "text": "Nairobi takes its name from a Maa phrase commonly translated as cool water. Maa spelling and pronunciation remain for speaker review.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Nairobi",
        "https://www.paukwa.or.ke/story-series/trackingourrailways/construction-of-the-railway-navigating-nairobi/",
        "https://nairobiassembly.go.ke/motion/provision-of-free-clean-drinking-water-in-hotels-and-other-public-areas/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "state": "documented",
      "text": "The Uganda Railway reached the site, 326 km from Mombasa, on 30 May 1899; it became a supply depot, a camp for construction workers (many from India) and the railway's headquarters.",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.paukwa.or.ke/story-series/trackingourrailways/construction-of-the-railway-navigating-nairobi/",
        "Cybriwsky, R. A. (2013) Capital Cities around the World. ABC-CLIO, p. 303."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "state": "documented",
      "text": "An account in Leakey (1977), vol. 1, p. 20, describes Kikuyu traders taking banana flour to Maasai traders in exchange for sheep. Printed-page confirmation remains pending; it does not establish a universal exchange rate.",
      "sources": [
        "Leakey, L. S. B. (1977) The Southern Kikuyu before 1903. Academic Press, vol. 1, p. 20."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "state": "documented",
      "text": "Kikuyu and Maasai areas of settlement and economic activity overlapped; trade and intermarriage connected them. This does not establish the fictional bargain or friendship in this story.",
      "sources": [
        "Muriuki, G. (1974) A History of the Kikuyu 1500-1900. Oxford University Press.",
        "https://soas-repository.worktribe.com/output/402032/a-history-of-the-kikuyu-to-1904",
        "Overton, J. (1988) 'The Origins of the Kikuyu Land Problem: Land Alienation and Land Use in Kiambu, Kenya, 1895-1920', African Studies Review 31(2). https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/african-studies-review/article/origins-of-the-kikuyu-land-problem-land-alienation-and-land-use-in-kiambu-kenya-18951920/01BDC3BCB4D2A9AC40F00F52500F1916"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "state": "documented",
      "text": "The Maasai milk gourd (ekukuri) was smoked inside with oloirien wood.",
      "sources": [
        "https://artsandculture.google.com/story/_gUx7TIiep4KKA"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C14",
      "state": "documented",
      "text": "Sankofa is an Akan word meaning, literally, 'go back and get it'.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sankofa",
        "https://nmaahc.si.edu/object/nmaahc_2014.321",
        "https://www.berea.edu/centers/carter-g-woodson-center-for-interracial-education/the-power-of-sankofa"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C15",
      "state": "documented",
      "text": "Community groups along the Nairobi River have undertaken waste removal, urban farming and restoration of public green spaces. UN-Habitat’s October 2024 river-walk account documents these efforts and remaining pollution. This does not identify the fictional garden as a real project or imply the entire river has been restored.",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.unhabitatyouth.org/en/a-transect-through-nairobis-past-present-and-future-outlook-river-walk-for-urban-regeneration/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "R1",
      "state": "reconstruction",
      "text": "Wambui and Naserian are fictional characters. Their meeting, family needs, earlier waiting, words, deferred payment, bead gift and inclusion of the visitor are imagined; none establishes a cultural custom, documented event or the visitor's ancestry."
    },
    {
      "id": "R2",
      "state": "reconstruction",
      "text": "The present-day riverside garden, the bead on its gate and any future visitor are imagined. They do not identify a real restoration project or descendants of the fictional women."
    }
  ],
